.TH ASSERT 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
assert \- a CI policy: these facts must hold about this binary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B assert
.I binary | bundle
.RI [ assertions ...]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Checks a set of assertions about one binary and exits 0 only when every one
holds. A release gate: "fail if symbol X is present, or string Y is missing".
.PP
.B audit
gates a file on its
.I internal
consistency; this gates it on facts the caller supplies. A build usually wants
both.
.SH ASSERTIONS
Four predicates, repeatable, each taking a value:
.TP
.BI \-\-has\-symbol= NAME
A symbol of exactly this name must be present, defined or imported.
.TP
.BI \-\-no\-symbol= NAME
No symbol of this name may be present.
.TP
.BI \-\-has\-string= TEXT
Some NUL\-terminated string must contain TEXT.
.TP
.BI \-\-no\-string= TEXT
No NUL\-terminated string may contain TEXT.
.PP
.B \-\-has\-symbol
and
.B \-\-no\-symbol
match the
.I whole
name: a policy names a symbol, and a substring would pass on
.B _main_helper
when asked about
.BR _main .
.B \-\-has\-string
and
.B \-\-no\-string
match a
.IR substring ,
because the useful claim is that a URL or an error message is present and the
string around it is not the point. Both string reads are the ones
.B findliteral \-\-strings
performs, over the same sections, so a string the listing shows is a string
this can assert.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Check one architecture by name:
.BR x86_64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
.BR arm64_32 ,
.BR ppc ,
.BR ppc64 ,
.BR arm
or
.BR i386 .
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read. May also be given as the first positional argument.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every assertion held.
.TP
.B 1
At least one did not. A
.I negative answer ,
not an error: a build gate needs to branch on "the policy failed", and a caller
that reads that as a crash will switch the gate off within a week.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including a flag with no value or an empty one.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
assert build/Contents/MacOS/app \e
    \-\-has\-symbol=_main \e
    \-\-no\-symbol=_NSLog \e
    \-\-has\-string="https://"
assert \-\-json build/Contents/MacOS/app \-\-no\-string=__debugSummary
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
A failed assertion is data, not an error:
.B errors
stays empty and
.B data.passed
is the verdict, the same shape
.BR audit (1)
returns. Only an unreadable file is an error.
.PP
An empty value is rejected rather than run.
.B \-\-has\-string ""
is true of every binary — the empty string is a substring of everything — so
accepting it would install a gate that can never fail, which is worse than no
gate because it reports success.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR audit (1),
.BR findliteral (1),
.BR sym (1),
.BR fingerprint (1)
